=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 32\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2.8 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=-2.8+2x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369762 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f33827e6-fbe3-5ac0-81bb-afc0b60f6ceb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3+4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369764} {:answer/id #uuid "69f66b87-8a13-5240-a11e-f3b29f44f14d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4+3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369765} {:answer/id #uuid "15d294b4-85e9-5347-9018-98bd1409800c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369766} {:answer/id #uuid "b5774131-ea9b-5ca6-bb51-7a928a8d2bb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369767} {:answer/id #uuid "f267db8f-0f38-543e-b961-caf64449125d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4+3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369765} :answer-field/id #uuid "8078f01b-e510-57a4-8b30-017102a3b605" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369763}] :question/id #uuid "3afd6077-78d7-5f4e-b464-aa77715a7491" :question/math-academy-id "q-139642" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $4$ | $7$ | $10$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}22 \\\\ 26\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}22 \\\\ 26\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y=4+3x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551291 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02cb7704-5d65-5a8a-b6f4-5c1f2eca8d99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3+6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550379} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb23ae6-3d85-59f7-9aa5-38c1889a0c20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550476} {:answer/id #uuid "767ed063-1f3b-5581-a169-3766dae98d5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-6-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550666} {:answer/id #uuid "90a442b4-496e-5aff-8488-442c3527f982" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-6+3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550735} {:answer/id #uuid "ee44493c-7944-5d36-ba66-2bc4ba331705" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3+6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550476} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b4192aa-858b-5977-ae07-cf74a154f636" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551111}] :question/id #uuid "776ef62d-2c09-5e2b-a7f2-e7cbc949af5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-139644" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $10$ | $2$ | $-4$ | $-8$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -30\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -30\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y=-3-6x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551310 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3414a2fb-1c40-5baf-b160-d62aa452c159" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2+4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550495} {:answer/id #uuid "580265ce-7676-517c-8e36-74524881eba6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550583} {:answer/id #uuid "6f203b32-367e-5763-8e6c-d666dfea11ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550646} {:answer/id #uuid "b301229a-92bc-5022-a48f-18260e3a02d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2+4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550855} {:answer/id #uuid "e41db3bb-d1d9-5070-ae1b-2af09a13aaa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550646} :answer-field/id #uuid "b80c480a-b5ef-5b14-b066-cb5d8f7268c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551178}] :question/id #uuid "95655e19-4263-5e26-a218-4871ce1b1f2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-139645" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-3$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $9$ | $6$ | $3$ | $-2$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{56}[\\begin{aligned}14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -28\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{56}[\\begin{aligned}14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -28\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y=4-2x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557260 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19864df4-2d2b-59b7-8b29-57ccd76568d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3.5-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556484} {:answer/id #uuid "2ffa906f-87d9-5316-b4b0-5c81c75eda13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3.5-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556532} {:answer/id #uuid "c73853c3-d2b8-5386-8f71-11b0dfc356a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1+3.5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556909} {:answer/id #uuid "ca337b13-8de7-56cb-a6fd-dfe6db160da2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1-3.5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556914} {:answer/id #uuid "d6194152-573b-5c08-a9b7-e672113befaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3.5+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3.5+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556951} :answer-field/id #uuid "32652aaf-8a9a-5672-9fc6-21a88c374b1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557096}] :question/id #uuid "685118c5-b7fa-5fb3-b5f9-06f8a56c7a87" :question/math-academy-id "q-139647" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-3$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $2$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{104}[\\begin{aligned}26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 26\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{104}[\\begin{aligned}26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ 26\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3.5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y=3.5+x.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133805 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133614 :question/id #uuid "a763a84a-0727-58a3-b524-04afa5735706" :question/math-academy-id "e-5611" :question/problem "An experiment consists of measuring how much a spring stretches (in inches) when one end of the spring is attached to the ceiling while a mass of $x$ ounces hangs from the other end. This data is shown below.\n\n| Mass $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Stretch $(y)$ | $0.8$ | $1.8$ | $3.2$ | $4.2$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, how much will the spring stretch if we use an $8$-ounce mass, assuming that the trend continues?  \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that* \n![](images/40/400621e90f0419646dcd9e2b2e1bc31bc3cc280bef2be6c3d330c8b69308429c.png)\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 \\\\ 1.8 \\\\ 3.2 \\\\ 4.2\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X^T} X )^{-1}$ and $X^T \\mathbf{y}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}23 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 38.2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.04 \\\\ 0.82\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y = 0.04+ 0.82x.$\n\nFinally, to predict how much the spring will stretch if an $8$-ounce mass is hung from it,  we substitute $x=8$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.04+0.82(8) \\\\  & =6.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, according to the model, if an $8$ ounce mass is hung on the spring, the spring will stretch $6.6$ inches. \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=8$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd286741-bf82-5723-a9c1-acf7a8b238c2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134111 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "979c5af4-9964-538b-b144-532063ca3f7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130823} {:answer/id #uuid "d9d790f1-d20b-5c8a-bbc1-23c707806914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130824} {:answer/id #uuid "2cf336cd-ac26-5998-9be3-a638605f8e3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8.5$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130825} {:answer/id #uuid "64aa2687-8d50-568b-9155-b17f093ad1c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9.5$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130826} {:answer/id #uuid "7d17029a-45a5-5838-89f1-19c40f3f3fb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7.5$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130827}] :answer-field/id #uuid "bb67d2ca-15d7-5506-b1d1-9f75b40c28f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130822} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c12a9093-8b33-506e-8833-5f1ad6a9261f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9.5$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400834} {:answer/id #uuid "032b222d-40e4-532e-af62-dfdc2dcc37e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400835} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce327d5-8771-5e11-b6ab-ff3533542dc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8.5$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400836} {:answer/id #uuid "e7d9746c-39bb-5726-8a32-8963780d412e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7.5$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400837} {:answer/id #uuid "a99677dd-5b51-522d-a3d5-8b761133f62a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400835} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc15713a-b900-5d20-8415-c6b1dd1f9e84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400833}] :question/id #uuid "8f426df0-1f38-5a2e-b701-5e427a0357ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-139750" :question/problem "A biologist has recorded data on a pine tree's age (in years) and height (in feet). This data is shown below. \n\n| Age $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Height $(y)$ | $1.2$ | $2.5$ | $3.6$ | $4.5$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the tree's height after $8$ years, assuming the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}15 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}11.8 \\\\ 35\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1.2 \\\\ 2.5 \\\\ 3.6 \\\\ 4.5\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}15 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}11.8 \\\\ 35\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 \\\\ 1.1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.2+1.1x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the  height after $8$ years, we substitute $x=8$ into the equation of the line: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.2+1.1(8) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, according to the model, the height of a pine tree that is $8$ years old will be $9$ feet. \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=8$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134362 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9949e4b7-78dc-54e1-b1e2-6cc17d5fdb53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$9200$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132212} {:answer/id #uuid "bdfe35c5-c31d-517c-8491-ee60cd035209" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$10400$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132213} {:answer/id #uuid "ed8de521-183c-5b4c-8a34-a2af3d37fe8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$12200$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132214} {:answer/id #uuid "0e37fc32-c14e-5987-8701-bf2b47456c68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$11500$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132215} {:answer/id #uuid "3f07dfd0-be79-5868-bcec-3c18ae2a9bd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$11300$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132216}] :answer-field/id #uuid "891df652-ad00-5c9b-a50d-52740ac80a98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132211} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "481e34c4-8e23-5638-8364-a626e241a934" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$10\\,400$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370921} {:answer/id #uuid "f52f14f2-3d6a-50fd-9ea7-988fb8ad0119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$12\\,200$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370922} {:answer/id #uuid "bedb7f6d-404b-5d77-ac8f-a59e15f52f92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$11\\,500$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370923} {:answer/id #uuid "d21b58ed-a896-595c-927e-75a0413683ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$9\\,200$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370924} {:answer/id #uuid "e75bad89-4049-573b-b220-b46d90025c2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$11\\,300$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$11\\,300$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370925} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5153227-95be-5d76-8614-6897e17ccc0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370920}] :question/id #uuid "378d2a03-3c23-5c86-9c0e-a7a3f421ff89" :question/math-academy-id "q-41742" :question/problem "A store collected data about its profits (in thousands of dollars) for the first five months since opening. This data is shown below.\n\n| Month $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $8.3$ | $5.1$ | $10.9$ | $10.7$ | $9.5$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the profit in the $6$th month, assuming that the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}44.5 \\\\ 141.5\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}8.3 \\\\ 5.1 \\\\ 10.9 \\\\ 10.7 \\\\ 9.5\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}44.5 \\\\ 141.5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}6.5 \\\\ 0.8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=6.5+0.8x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the profit in the $6$th month, we substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =6.5+0.8(6) \\\\  & =11.3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the profit in the $6$th month will be $$11\\,300.$\n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=6$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271966 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2259a17f-5cc0-5255-86c8-c5648372ce89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2\\,800$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269564} {:answer/id #uuid "352e371a-5116-55e8-823a-4d8ea16c5f5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$4\\,400$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269756} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec6dbc9-9c7e-5f08-b5b7-2523fa7d0328" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$3\\,700$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270377} {:answer/id #uuid "db0484e6-fc73-549b-aacc-acee23620cda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$4\\,200$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271187} {:answer/id #uuid "f31f859d-103d-5d18-8576-c162b1f1e5ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$3\\,200$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$3\\,200$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271403} :answer-field/id #uuid "26e892f1-6a97-51e5-8f0d-5824fa06ea60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271581}] :question/id #uuid "2a70d052-0349-524f-90c0-17395372863f" :question/math-academy-id "q-139735" :question/problem "A company collected data on the sale price (in thousands of dollars) of a particular used car model and the car's age (in years). This data is shown below.\n\n| Age $(x)$ | **$4$** | **$5$** | **$6$** | **$7$** | **$8$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Price $(y)$ | $12$ | $10$ | $8.5$ | $7$ | $6.5$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the sale price of a model that is $10$ years old, assuming the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}38 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}44 \\\\ 250\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 8.5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 6.5\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}38 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}44 \\\\ 250\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}17.2 \\\\ -1.4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=17.2-1.4x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the selling price of a car of the same model that is $10$ years old, we substitute $x=10$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =17.2-1.4(10) \\\\  & =3.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the sale price will be $$3\\,200.$\n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=10$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271967 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c0dffe5-8c18-5c35-922b-ab2349cf0224" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$826$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270230} {:answer/id #uuid "775e536f-38c2-5ab0-af53-9c42718f6442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$868$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270326} {:answer/id #uuid "93d8e9e4-fad7-52e1-8058-4325cb48029f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$906$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270562} {:answer/id #uuid "bc89e73c-4770-571b-b13d-e6698523a25d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$902$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270927} {:answer/id #uuid "fc3d4783-4f4a-52b1-a174-c83258ea7302" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$872$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$906$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270562} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e4a8479-bce9-519b-bc40-a906989c59fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271789}] :question/id #uuid "51f93e5f-4b65-5909-b2fb-547fe7d03d90" :question/math-academy-id "q-139736" :question/problem "A statistician collected data on the height above the ground (in hundreds of meters) of a hot air balloon during the first five minutes of its flight. This data is shown below. \n\n| Time $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Height $(y)$ | $1.6$ | $3.0$ | $4.6$ | $6$ | $7.6$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the balloon's height after $6$ minutes, assuming the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}22.8 \\\\ 83.4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1.6 \\\\ 3.0 \\\\ 4.6 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 7.6\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}22.8 \\\\ 83.4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.06 \\\\ 1.5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.06+1.5x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the height of the balloon after $6$ minutes, we substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.06+1.5(6) \\\\  & =9.06\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the balloon's height after $6$ minutes will be $906$ meters.  \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=6$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326903 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c93ebea-33d6-548b-b12a-3b0fefae0570" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$29.8$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324462} {:answer/id #uuid "8498f4d4-ec28-50d0-9f84-9f3de7e32279" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$27.9$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325089} {:answer/id #uuid "a29c3ff4-17c9-526e-8584-e74c4fe900af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$28.5$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325349} {:answer/id #uuid "e4c65f91-7aa7-5d92-8d0d-ede3fefd70ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$28$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325987} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9b2db0-1ba5-5c3f-a06f-113ecc0be69a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$29$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$29.8$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324462} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac702602-2c74-56ec-b3c7-12f968e683f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326533}] :question/id #uuid "3832f676-5ec9-5b13-afe4-f7324769e216" :question/math-academy-id "q-41467" :question/problem "The relationship between the advertising budget and sales (both in millions of dollars) of a company per year over several years is shown in the following table. \n\n| Advertising $(x)$ | **$2$** | **$2.5$** | **$3$** | **$3.5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Sales $(y)$ | $10$ | $12$ | $16$ | $20$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the total sales when the annual advertising budget is equal to $$5$ million, assuming the trend continues? \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}31.5 & -11 \\\\ -11 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}58 \\\\ 168\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2.5 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3.5\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}31.5 & -11 \\\\ -11 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}58 \\\\ 168\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4.2 \\\\ 6.8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=-4.2+6.8x.$ \n\nFinally, to predict the value of the sales when the advertising budget is equal to $$5$ million, we substitute $x=5$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-4.2+6.8(5) \\\\  & =29.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the total sales when the advertising budget equals $$5$ million will be $$29.8$ million. \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=5$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400826 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24daf051-a969-5853-92c4-9336c99d9eac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.75$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400828} {:answer/id #uuid "b8c5c0b9-e64a-579b-9d0a-5faca271c7fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5.15$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400829} {:answer/id #uuid "b25e0551-5bff-5a95-b67a-7ec0fc2e22ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.25$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400830} {:answer/id #uuid "4166a757-6ff8-5e2d-b4fe-2e6fc3e044b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.25$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400831} {:answer/id #uuid "00805968-8b23-52f9-a5f4-d3708a27362f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.75$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.75$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400828} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4b23bec-c550-5bbf-bae4-d3fc66059766" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400827}] :question/id #uuid "3ba26ac4-59dd-55c3-b644-94e5b96251c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-139749" :question/problem "A bungee jumping instructor measures how much a bungee rope stretches (in meters) when one end of the rope is attached to the bridge railing while a weight of $x$ kilograms is hung on the other. This data is shown below.\n\n| Weight $(x)$ | **$10$** | **$20$** | **$30$** | **$40$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Stretch $(y)$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $2.5$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, how much will the rope stretch if the instructor uses a $65$-kilogram mass, assuming the trend continues?  \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that* \n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{500}\\begin{bmatrix}750 & -25 \\\\ -25 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 200\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 10 \\\\ 1 & 20 \\\\ 1 & 30 \\\\ 1 & 40\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1.5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2.5\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{500}\\begin{bmatrix}750 & -25 \\\\ -25 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 200\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 \\\\ 0.05\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.5+0.05x.$\n\nFinally, to predict how much the rope will stretch if a $65$-kilogram mass is hung from it, we substitute $x=65$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.5+0.05(65) \\\\  & =3.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the rope will stretch $3.75$ meters if a $65$-kilogram mass is hung from it.\n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=65$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402736 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e9ac6ff-6dc0-50d1-bcf0-e5924b445f0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$1\\,050$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402738} {:answer/id #uuid "fc84303a-bedd-5991-9237-200188eab34c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$950$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402739} {:answer/id #uuid "dc47e4af-c36a-5241-a8ef-baaa45ef2c51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$875$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402740} {:answer/id #uuid "21e67319-25a2-5a84-a939-d04c90f738c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$900$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402741} {:answer/id #uuid "82ab20d8-e65e-5a0a-ba4c-f261ab35ac4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$850$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402742}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$900$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402741} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba3cb994-1bad-5429-8732-d77a5dada295" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402737}] :question/id #uuid "d0007be5-4b39-5b13-9d0c-f56e380ce0f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-41741" :question/problem "A small business collected data on the number of hours of telephone usage and the total amount (in hundreds of dollars) of the company's weekly operating expenses. This data is shown below.   \n\n| Hours $(x)$ | **$2$** | **$2.4$** | **$2.8$** | **$3.2$** | **$3.6$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Expenses $(y)$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the company's weekly operating expenses if the telephone is used for a total of $4$ hours per week, assuming the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}40.8 & -14 \\\\ -14 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}30 \\\\ 88\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2.4 \\\\ 1 & 2.8 \\\\ 1 & 3.2 \\\\ 1 & 3.6\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\begin{bmatrix}40.8 & -14 \\\\ -14 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}30 \\\\ 88\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2.5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=-1+2.5x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the amount of the company's weekly operating expenses if the telephone is used for a total of $4$ hours, we substitute $x=4$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1+2.5(4) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the company's weekly operating expenses will be $$900.$ \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=4$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402743 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ba73043-ba7f-5144-85cc-553d87d248c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$24\\,600$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402745} {:answer/id #uuid "1584ef53-01f2-5061-8e58-39b6ee6c83df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$37\\,300$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402746} {:answer/id #uuid "7cfaef46-165b-54fb-98ec-40334338b6c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$20\\,400$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402747} {:answer/id #uuid "01465c5f-aea0-556f-b1a7-2a55b583c3af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$34\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402748} {:answer/id #uuid "3a5f7e29-cc12-59e3-bba6-2cee48d6debc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$39\\,200$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$20\\,400$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402747} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd4ac2e5-74a0-528f-8078-d6aa415b8f9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402744}] :question/id #uuid "e98728e8-7708-5748-ae2b-39ed7242bc51" :question/math-academy-id "q-41751" :question/problem "A store collected data about its profits (in thousands of dollars) for the year's first five months. This data is shown below.\n\n| Month $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $12$ | $15$ | $16$ | $15$ | $20$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the profit in the $6$th month, assuming the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}78 \\\\ 250\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}78 \\\\ 250\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}10.8 \\\\ 1.6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=10.8+1.6x.$ \n\nFinally, to predict the profit in the $6$th month, we substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =10.8+1.6(6) \\\\  & =20.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the profit in the $6$th month will be $$20\\,400.$\n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=6$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551239 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0231280f-3c96-52d4-bb02-f31de91a72d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$44.7$ inches" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550376} {:answer/id #uuid "2e066363-c73f-5f32-81f2-579f95f3d955" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$45.8$  inches" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550482} {:answer/id #uuid "54b64262-45e4-5112-8f5a-007f27ab1984" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$46.9$ inches" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550568} {:answer/id #uuid "d868415f-f29e-51af-aefa-fa2b3c4c46cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$45.1$  inches" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550953} {:answer/id #uuid "e78f56b5-9629-515c-a62c-674b58e812ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$46.2$  inches" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$45.1$  inches" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550953} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cbcd786-caa3-5353-bd0b-1dabaa09f399" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551139}] :question/id #uuid "1515bb33-4938-5f32-8710-f2dc1f06ddad" :question/math-academy-id "q-41739" :question/problem "A physics experiment consists of a small object moving along a straight line. The following table contains data collected on the distance traveled by the object (in inches) during the first five seconds. \n\n| Time $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Distance $(y)$ | $8.6$ | $13.1$ | $24.6$ | $28.1$ | $38.6$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what distance will the object cover after $6$ seconds, assuming the trend continues? \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}113 \\\\ 414\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}8.6 \\\\ 13.1 \\\\ 24.6 \\\\ 28.1 \\\\ 38.6\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}113 \\\\ 414\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 7.5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.1+7.5x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the distance the object will cover after $6$ seconds, we substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.1+7.5(6) \\\\  & =45.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the object will cover a distance of $45.1$ inches in $6$ seconds.  \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=6$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551337 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0334f116-416e-5d14-b3bf-b66d4f75de11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8.25$  points per game" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550380} {:answer/id #uuid "33db2298-5797-5cb1-af3d-bf806c19e083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7.75$  points per game" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550493} {:answer/id #uuid "bd226916-bde7-517b-871e-7e8629dd5812" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$  points per game" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550887} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b10de6-86fa-53ca-94d7-3e008b6bfb17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$  points per game" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550935} {:answer/id #uuid "f5961ae6-fa3a-550a-9ffb-b71a8701df1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8.75$  points per game" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$  points per game" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550887} :answer-field/id #uuid "5784eef9-0ad2-5040-8dd4-135f8ec74797" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551117}] :question/id #uuid "d6dbf98c-84c4-5c67-a15a-4f405cbbd0b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-41401" :question/problem "The height (in feet) and the average number of points scored per game (APG) of four players on a basketball team are shown in the following table. \n\n| Height $(x)$ | **$6$** | **$6.5$** | **$6.5$** | **$7$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| APG $(y)$ | $5$ | $8$ | $10$ | $10$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the average number of points scored per game by a player whose height is $6.45\\,\\text{ft}?$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}84.75 & -13 \\\\ -13 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}33 \\\\ 217\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 6.5 \\\\ 1 & 6.5 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}84.75 & -13 \\\\ -13 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}33 \\\\ 217\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-24.25 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=-24.25+5x.$ \n\nFinally, to predict the average number of points scored per game by a player whose height is $6.45\\,\\text{ft},$  we substitute $x=6.45$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-24.25+5(6.45) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model,  the average number of points scored per game by a player whose height is $6.45\\,\\text{ft}$ will be $8$  points per game."}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line in Context"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133905 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133718 :question/id #uuid "29b2a4d7-7e88-5f7a-9a84-ff5315c38f92" :question/math-academy-id "e-9914" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-6$** | **$-3$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-7$ | $-4$ | $2$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients  of the line $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ that best fits the data above can be found using linear regression as \n\n![](images/80/8002d38943a0f0b72e5c9cef2e3a455915479ca86c53ebe403b60a6b49f268a4.png)\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. Which are the design matrix $X$ and the observation vector $\\mathbf{y}$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$\\color{blue}-6$** | **$\\color{blue}-3$** | **$\\color{blue}-1$** | **$\\color{blue}0$** | **$\\color{blue}4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $\\color{red}-7$ | $\\color{red}-4$ | $\\color{red}2$ | $\\color{red}8$ | $\\color{red}10$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6acfe386-f7e4-54b1-9701-6ff5905b4536" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134106 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b20594c2-8b13-5743-9418-00ca234bb530" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -5 & -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130793} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c59f9d-b512-54e7-9cb1-7c7e2fe4dae6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130794} {:answer/id #uuid "d83d477f-73fa-5372-b7a8-321ce9811cd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130795} {:answer/id #uuid "f1374cf2-7b4c-577f-9f53-fda2e786227d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130796} {:answer/id #uuid "19f0bcaf-a2eb-5f63-bfcc-39ae986682d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130797}] :answer-field/id #uuid "47431566-66ec-59a7-a8b0-d58094c893d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130792} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42f36ce8-abaa-5341-9ce7-36023c59f115" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400758} {:answer/id #uuid "da4f77a0-7473-5225-8669-46210814431f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400759} {:answer/id #uuid "417fc55a-2009-510a-bab5-565815a9b746" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -5 & -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400760} {:answer/id #uuid "0d357aa1-84b3-5747-9e58-2031587733f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400761} {:answer/id #uuid "16b4b488-c57d-56fe-8fb4-7e5235fe25dc" :answer/type {:db/ident