se is $\\sqrt{11}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493911 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "102ee921-b567-5226-bf65-b160183a7595" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493419} {:answer/id #uuid "8e986c86-71b7-5a51-a24e-17131a654ca7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493616} {:answer/id #uuid "9edcd302-0ef2-5f93-959d-3aceccbb61b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493638} {:answer/id #uuid "a0b46f98-5ac8-5b4e-bb94-ccb2f5d2c140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493646} {:answer/id #uuid "fbbf704b-a180-57cb-8ffd-3202258def89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493638} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ef5407b-1455-54e1-b51b-9e80211017fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493813}] :question/id #uuid "39aef628-7059-5436-8f38-6e05c93c2909" :question/math-academy-id "q-139506" :question/problem "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $13$ and $8,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $13,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}=13\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=\\sqrt{13}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{13}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493930 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d697f1a-91d4-5ea3-899b-c3408ee2ab1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493413} {:answer/id #uuid "54d38533-d806-50e5-a7f3-9d141a602fc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493521} {:answer/id #uuid "c87c11da-4ee7-5c82-9fac-22443d8cbc9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493713} {:answer/id #uuid "d56c67fa-abc8-5c4a-9511-24fe90b45abb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493727} {:answer/id #uuid "e21caf68-2b09-5596-859d-4d5d49e08b4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493727} :answer-field/id #uuid "756b7007-2354-5f57-a27e-e64df3e652a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493836}] :question/id #uuid "6b27cf61-4032-5d19-a7e8-2cfee6fa397a" :question/math-academy-id "q-139531" :question/problem "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $12$ and $7,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $12,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}=12\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $2\\sqrt{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559792 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7060a51a-f1ac-56cc-8649-47d79f80e66a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559295} {:answer/id #uuid "743c9f6c-4604-5a5e-ae09-e46410e9b736" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559309} {:answer/id #uuid "83008559-0106-50b8-aade-d8999902ca44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559342} {:answer/id #uuid "9a03575c-0bbb-5f48-9976-7364f8857c77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559405} {:answer/id #uuid "a57ac798-2d4f-569f-8786-634addc82e29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559295} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c64eaf9-aef9-5ec3-ab69-6953be3e0926" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559722}] :question/id #uuid "352fc2e3-10d1-5d70-be93-5831359ed522" :question/math-academy-id "q-139527" :question/problem "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $11,$ $9,$ and $0,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $11,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}=11\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=\\sqrt{11}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{11}.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162098 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161911 :question/id #uuid "06ca6e76-6354-5450-882e-b0c063acd303" :question/math-academy-id "e-9908" :question/problem "![](images/09/094b6043b9cce7f3ac2a7cc32fa53785d3fbf5b490ce853474f89bb4afa8291a.png)\n\nA $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^2$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ are unit eigenvectors of $A^TA,$ find an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $36$ of $A^T A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\mathbf v_1, \\mathbf v_2\\,\\ldots, \\mathbf v_n\\right\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of $\\mathbb R^n$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $A^T A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1\\geq \\lambda_2\\geq \\ldots \\geq \\lambda_n.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- $\\sigma_1 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_1}$ is the length of $A\\mathbf v_1,$\n\n- $\\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{\\lambda_2}$ is the length of $A\\mathbf v_2,$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A\\mathbf v_1|| = 8 = \\sigma_1,$ which means that $\\lambda_1 = 8^2 = 64,$ and\n\n- $||A\\mathbf v_2|| = 6 = \\sigma_2,$ which means that $\\lambda_2 = 6^2 = 36.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_2 = 36$ of $A^T A$ is $\\mathbf{v}_2.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cec3c21-28df-52ba-9b78-86cffb4c7720" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162362 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4adfe18a-fa58-5f98-9ac3-e41976c7232c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159377} {:answer/id #uuid "57323d56-0255-5884-8e73-45ea1c7fcac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$16$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159378} {:answer/id #uuid "6d9066f0-4a14-571e-b7a0-ef918b097a43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159379} {:answer/id #uuid "38f9c426-dab0-57b7-aa76-6f687589b344" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4{v}_{1}$ and $2{v}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564065} {:answer/id #uuid "585850a0-2e98-5454-a5ce-c2c74b29b565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159379} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddea8248-482b-5038-b9ed-6aad287810e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159374}] :question/id #uuid "ba175b74-8169-5d32-9cc8-7cf8e202ba3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-139420" :question/problem "![](images/93/931f7cc1fe4cc6a45c1f586bb017bb3eda8f7745aa3973c13f08aa8521c39400.png)\n\nA $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ what are the nonzero singular values of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=4={\\sigma }_{1},$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=2={\\sigma }_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=4$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162363 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27612a52-9a44-5194-990a-b67bd5b0cb3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266495} {:answer/id #uuid "3781afe7-ca23-5caf-8d77-e210ce87765d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266623} {:answer/id #uuid "9cbfef3a-23de-5494-b267-533971519fee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267552} {:answer/id #uuid "a48b2a55-e19f-5a3b-aad5-9311f9e9ad3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267628} {:answer/id #uuid "a9e9b4e6-ef6f-5008-83b9-4dc476b26261" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{v}_{1}+5{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267628} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f73eb28-9edf-5454-bbc8-1e8090f60bd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268549}] :question/id #uuid "14414eae-18ed-5455-a3d4-1f9724606970" :question/math-academy-id "q-139421" :question/problem "![](images/f2/f26e876d5234313926a2cbaa8938e32c8a725cf3435ce3113361bfa3dc1b7997.png)\n\nA $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ which of the following is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $25$ of ${A}^{T}A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=7={\\sigma }_{1},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{1}={7}^{2}=49,$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=5={\\sigma }_{2},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{2}={5}^{2}=25.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=25$ of ${A}^{T}A$ is ${v}_{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269244 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2380798b-79b8-5bc8-8153-92b706b0d629" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266470} {:answer/id #uuid "aa5c7d7d-7d57-554b-8590-31cd03b7bf50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267684} {:answer/id #uuid "bd57370c-6b04-52dc-acbe-5e2a687609ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$16$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267839} {:answer/id #uuid "cca90f51-cb57-5f81-bcb5-412e125542b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267976} {:answer/id #uuid "f862619f-07c7-5287-9b92-80801fdc6ea1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8{v}_{1}$ and $5{v}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267684} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c9e107d-8a8c-55a2-9d3c-26e1ff04f4aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268633}] :question/id #uuid "f71d275b-8f83-5bf3-90f9-5e8ed34d76ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-139547" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d4947e37239d2ac2950b536d7afc2d0810ea713c437b7095a43e123d50ff2b34.png)\n\nA $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ what are the nonzero singular values of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=8={\\sigma }_{1},$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=5={\\sigma }_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=8$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269245 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27e6efca-95b5-5f7f-84df-c43898904f74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7{v}_{1}$ and $5{v}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266499} {:answer/id #uuid "508bf2fb-93f6-5990-ad55-30bb910e8138" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266862} {:answer/id #uuid "6ded4070-afc4-5321-930c-7fff89731796" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267108} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c6b982-581f-5c4d-a40d-daef264ebab6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267735} {:answer/id #uuid "c94c857c-1f07-525f-b49b-1f0a26b0f150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{7}$ and $10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267108} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f8a517c-fc79-5b6f-8898-65042efeffb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268512}] :question/id #uuid "bfc4c8a3-4cfa-58b3-bd85-7a7059d385d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-139548" :question/problem "![](images/6b/6be1f51600ae7b42ad0c4c8f67eb8f3ba3570606ec4e3e5e75c65df397aab060.png)\n\nA $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ what are the nonzero singular values of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonorma