 ${\\lambda }_{2}={3}^{2}=9.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=9$ of ${A}^{T}A$ is ${v}_{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388618 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "220208ba-fab8-55a0-8a2f-2eb8fb671d38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388620} {:answer/id #uuid "5dde93b7-ebe5-5d5f-8618-0c649d764704" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388621} {:answer/id #uuid "d8598738-9382-5b1a-b55a-73d18649f6d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388622} {:answer/id #uuid "f6853d60-cd68-56ae-8530-5d5610a55167" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{v}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388623} {:answer/id #uuid "fce765ef-ed14-5c1c-a75f-4c9e2f5446a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{A}^{T}{v}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388621} :answer-field/id #uuid "e12f6b66-e0b5-5c3d-87c1-2792f81acfc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388619}] :question/id #uuid "82b609f1-ebbe-5986-a6ff-a556a13681d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-139549" :question/problem "![](images/7a/7a2aea092b82dd217b848abdfc162425f098bc13c53975ccfced3f60a4e2ebfb.png)\n\nA $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ which of the following is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $16$ of ${A}^{T}A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=8={\\sigma }_{1},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{1}={8}^{2}=64,$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=4={\\sigma }_{2},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{2}={4}^{2}=16.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=16$ of ${A}^{T}A$ is ${v}_{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400763 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72d8c257-9208-59a3-b8ef-e84226b5d7cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400765} {:answer/id #uuid "c56b5625-8f3f-5546-bd45-82c8dcf4f2c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$25$ and $9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400766} {:answer/id #uuid "9f361d5f-db40-531a-8f20-5389f05b015d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{7}$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400767} {:answer/id #uuid "c82a5223-3bd0-5bad-9e6b-edd5899707f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$11$ and $7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400768} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a6a4f9-ba6e-5a5b-9c4a-a88207909379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400765} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dac3c89-e776-5135-b836-fafd4c581933" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400764}] :question/id #uuid "925e17f0-1f91-564c-b150-3d5633a84c6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-139546" :question/problem "![](images/6d/6df3639cb222756221803446ebc735cf1e7cbcf4a922ed7fce835c0c44fb9aa1.png)\n\nA $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ what are the nonzero singular values of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=5={\\sigma }_{1},$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=3={\\sigma }_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400770 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47ade149-2321-54bc-8689-575d0021aa81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400772} {:answer/id #uuid "0322f6ef-5cd8-5b08-95d5-f8a6bea015f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400773} {:answer/id #uuid "56994b9b-ba0c-523d-9995-ad7e4cb025c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9{v}_{1}$ and $4{v}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400774} {:answer/id #uuid "caa9e423-3397-56e4-b07f-3555e9f94692" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400775} {:answer/id #uuid "cc058b2e-f11f-5130-a8ae-a5e6d87d4307" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$18$ and $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400772} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0b9d051-ab23-5d38-8461-85040e182756" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400771}] :question/id #uuid "f3e183c0-f2c5-57a2-8b6d-0c81e407e953" :question/math-academy-id "q-139550" :question/problem "![](images/57/57368434b3025dc777c5be725da43e365dfd8ef3c47049570c0ecb13f2baf035.png)\n\nA $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ what are the nonzero singular values of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=9={\\sigma }_{1},$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=4={\\sigma }_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems Regarding the Image of the Unit Eigenvectors of a Quadratic Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162099 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161912 :question/id #uuid "23c1671b-da2c-570b-af4f-c6aad258155d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9909" :question/problem "A $2 \\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto an ellipse in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are $\\sigma_1=4,$ $\\sigma_2=2,$ and $\\sigma_3 = 0,$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ onto the ellipse $\\big\\{ A\\mathbf{x} \\,|\\, \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1 \\big\\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $A\\mathbf{x}$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}^T (A^T A) \\mathbf{x}$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix $A^T A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| =1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix $A^T A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of $A^T A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is $\\sigma_1=4,$ the largest eigenvalue of $A^T A$ must be $\\lambda_1 = \\sigma_1^2=4^2.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\|^2 \\big\\} = 4^2\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\max\\limits_{\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1} \\big\\{ \\| A\\mathbf{x} \\| \\big\\} = 4.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sigma_1=4.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd0e91f0-abbc-52e2-8d8b-94597e4892cd" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162361 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dba961d-e303-56ab-9252-ba2464cee1cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266691} {:answer/id #uuid "4378e1d0-c8f3-5953-926f-a34f93bba17f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266747} {:answer/id #uuid "8d9bc846-a28a-56c0-bdb0-e96f8341147b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267407} {:answer/id #uuid "d02e44ed-9088-5422-8faa-e5a47f1b611c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268006} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b0a071-152b-5d66-8b17-d25aa4bb1b53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268347} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfe6c093-26dc-5d7a-ada3-01470001afa3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268802}] :question/id #uuid "cdd91aaf-b8dd-5936-93a2-ee3b24ba20f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-139418" :question/problem "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=10$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{5},$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=10,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={10}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}={10}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=10.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=10.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162365 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "843730e5-1d6c-590a-be6f-f5bc76d354a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369715} {:answer/id #uuid "7d127bbd-7566-53f6-8abe-dfa396d7a93b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369716} {:answer/id #uuid "9b31420c-6449-52e6-a9be-475a6ec94b85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369717} {:answer/id #uuid "b308a8e3-0047-576e-8cfc-a0c1e309ab3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369718} {:answer/id #uuid "fdcdf069-d67b-5c89-b3b7-b34c02297e84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369716} :answer-field/id #uuid "12561a6a-dd60-5043-b724-be8966244b1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369714}] :question/id #uuid "55a74907-e699-576b-a088-5a1093017cc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-139507" :question/problem "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=3,$ ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{2},$ and ${\\sigma }_{3}=0,$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=3,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={3}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}={3}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=3.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269240 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a429996-164b-54fc-9946-577431de1ccb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266657} {:answer/id #uuid "4f3a6a58-573b-5732-bf74-6c0a1ac7d701" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266849} {:answer/id #uuid "62be0fb0-0dcb-57b3-9f91-1d3ab410a49b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267012} {:answer/id #uuid "afc186cf-aab9-589d-af44-efe97c49758b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267725} {:answer/id #uuid "cd7691e1-a3bb-53c8-9a1f-15a33a8deed3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266657} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a4b6387-6d51-5a91-bc9a-e1d31b9ff78c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268474}] :question/id #uuid "ed90c3b2-491c-5a1e-a27e-a0b50d59ad00" :question/math-academy-id "q-139535" :question/problem "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{3},$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=9,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={9}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}={9}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=9.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=9.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269241 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "157ee7b9-2a37-56af-a675-3b93033c19d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266326} {:answer/id #uuid "2316ce20-3b2d-54fa-8fb0-522d6f775571" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266463} {:answer/id #uuid "50d91456-6bd8-520e-8f76-eaa4803ace5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266865} {:answer/id #uuid "79a0bc50-a5c1-5d32-be4c-51c1e0ade439" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267218} {:answer/id #uuid "e92173af-f53d-58d