{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}={8}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=8.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=8.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383417 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e50d71c6-f44a-5378-9963-36734ea464d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383419} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ca5674-a813-571b-805e-2032f3f85b16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383420} {:answer/id #uuid "6475d879-742b-5337-8a8d-36ed47f16244" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383421} {:answer/id #uuid "400156c4-1064-54a3-9f5b-1121fe453b45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383422} {:answer/id #uuid "53eb8b86-520d-5c8a-ba6a-c003c2b2acaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383421} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d030424-2f49-5472-a91f-15160f4718d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383418}] :question/id #uuid "32d00c3f-b2c7-560e-b9fc-7da8d2b35ae5" :question/math-academy-id "q-139534" :question/problem "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=7$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{5},$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=7,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={7}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}={7}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=7.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=7.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1c07e06-038d-5f20-b7aa-6a49d66cccc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383426} {:answer/id #uuid "089c44cb-1411-5b96-a011-181bffcdb31b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383427} {:answer/id #uuid "0538b559-3b27-53cf-bf4b-40d7ba06c7ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383428} {:answer/id #uuid "7e08020a-0701-57c1-8997-c2b48f3e8f20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383429} {:answer/id #uuid "f1836749-28a7-5fbd-bd3f-d78ad98340b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383428} :answer-field/id #uuid "352c429f-c09f-51de-981a-03fd6b374654" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383425}] :question/id #uuid "b5c458b4-8436-5a2e-a1dd-0c56afc161a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-139540" :question/problem "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=7,$ ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{3},$ and ${\\sigma }_{3}=0,$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=7,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={7}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}={7}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=7.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=7.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493901 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b3b4afb-fa95-55b9-bb95-7bd1163684f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493463} {:answer/id #uuid "2c81dabb-dfd7-5bfa-a4eb-6f813166334c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493466} {:answer/id #uuid "9407fc80-2f45-5795-8b6d-8d17d520c654" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493621} {:answer/id #uuid "a13015bd-c5c8-578c-9c60-786d446af287" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493647} {:answer/id #uuid "ee940030-c583-5d51-8be9-b9494cf6a665" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493647} :answer-field/id #uuid "97f16011-48a5-5603-ae76-0cd52cee0497" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493849}] :question/id #uuid "236e6a34-eb12-52ba-848b-aa1ea35c9c6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-139533" :question/problem "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=6$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{3},$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=6,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={6}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}={6}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=6.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503755 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c5df5ba-75f0-5bfc-a30e-6793337660ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503195} {:answer/id #uuid "7d6dfca0-cb60-5c5b-ae49-7cd685619f7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503239} {:answer/id #uuid "87db152d-bf36-549b-bb17-dd5daccc80b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503259} {:answer/id #uuid "a7b73526-0075-5162-b576-f6193e008526" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503320} {:answer/id #uuid "f92b14f4-9494-5bbe-b406-714b16837483" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503239} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e35f8d5-2126-585d-9e2b-f8a2c67ed434" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503555}] :question/id #uuid "f2c3caea-6032-5d20-9fea-52c78472efb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-139538" :question/problem "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=8$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=3\\sqrt{3},$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=8,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={8}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}={8}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=8.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=8.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere Given the Corresponding Singular Values"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3131 :topic/title "The Singular Values of a Matrix"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222332} :activity/id #uuid "bf2387fe-ebed-5568-a260-0d681cb9fd28" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222332 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222502 :tutorial/content "Consider the homogeneous linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}' = A\\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix.\n\nA **fundamental matrix** for this system is an $n\\times n$ matrix $\\Phi(t)$ whose columns are $n$ linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIf $\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\dots,\\mathbf{x}_n(t)$ are linearly independent solutions, then one fundamental matrix is\n\n$$\n\\Phi(t)=\\big[\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\dots,\\mathbf{x}_n(t)\\big].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $\\Phi(t)$ can be written in any order.\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "a7207a24-194d-59d6-b25c-f9bb4f1b0d6e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14508 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3b0bd285-b383-5ebb-821f-4f74a0b37719" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222333}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222333 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221420 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af871f3d-da9e-5fd3-a61f-94e155ccf2b3"} :step/id #uuid "aa3c7790-3ab9-5519-bad3-4f82fc8ac113" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222334}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222334 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222503 :tutorial/content "Again, let $\\Phi(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for the linear system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}' = A\\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix. The fundamental matrices have the following important properties.\n\n1\\. *Satisfies the underlying system*. Indeed, since each column of $\\Phi(t)$ is a solution of the system, we have\n\n  $$\n  \\Phi'(t) = A\\,\\Phi(t).\n  $$\n\n2\\. *Invertibility*. The columns of $\\Phi(t)$ are linearly independent for all $t,$ so\n\n  $$\n  \\det\\Phi(t)\\neq 0\n  $$\n\n  for all $t.$ In particular, $\\Phi(t)$ is invertible for all $t.$\n\n3\\. *Representation of solutions*. Every solution $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ of the system can be written in the form\n\n  $$\n  \\mathbf{x}(t)=\\Phi(t)\\,\\mathbf{c},\n  $$\n\n  where $\\mathbf{c}$ is a constant vector.\n\n4\\. *Non-uniqueness*. If $C$ is any invertible constant matrix, then\n\n  $$\n  \\Psi(t)=\\Phi(t)\\,C\n  $$\n\n  is also a fundamental matrix for the same system.\n\nWe'll now get some practice." :tutorial/id #uuid "e2fdbc29-4542-5fdc-adbd-316c5dcb6878" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14509 :tutorial/title "Properties of the Fundamental Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "2fe7252e-7fc7-59bb-bc32-74c452b7cf73" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222335}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222335 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221421 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0aea7503-74d6-5ba4-9411-ee53e59512d0"} :step/id #uuid "2b90cd12-9695-5b6b-b407-6eed6ae7fc84" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222336}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222336 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222504 :tutorial/content "For a linear system $\\mathbf{x}' = A\\mathbf{x}$ with constant $n\\times n$ matrix $A,$ the **standard fundamental matrix** is the unique fundamental matrix $\\Phi_0(t)$ that satisfies the initial condition\n\n$$\n\\Phi_0(0)=I_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $I_n$ is the $n \\times n$ identity matrix.\n\nFor constant matrices $A,$ the standard fundamental matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\Phi_0(t)=e^{At}.\n$$\n\nThis matrix satisfies\n\n$$\n\\Phi_0'(t)=A\\,\\Phi_0(t)\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\Phi_0(0)=I_n.\n$$\n\n**Note:** Any other fundamental matrix $\\Phi(t)$ for the same system can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\Phi(t)=\\Phi_0(t)\\,C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an invertible constant matrix.\n\nIn the next slide, we will compute the standard fundamental matrix for a specific example." :tutorial/id #uuid "4f75576c-be32-5784-9fee-e1288445728c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14510 :tutorial/title "Fundamental Matrices and Matrix Exponentials"} :step/id #uuid "d44c84d5-4215-5318-bba2-81eb3a34ee7d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222337}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222337 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221422 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66c99cda-25a5-50f2-903b-bfb1fdc5fa10"} :step/id #uuid "32e0e465-9256-5f88-b771-2edff24ecd54"}] :activity/title "Fundamental Matrices" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186221468} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498623} :activity/id #uuid "cfe34345-755d-5da2-bb57-2997badcd43f" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498607 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311648 :question/id #uuid "3d9725c1-fcc5-57a9-8b5a-bd5efbda8207"} :step/id #uuid "0d32e49b-f44f-5aaf-b893-9c5777c8ec8a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498653}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498619 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498522 :question/id #uuid "3fdf52f0-09f2-54b2-864b-627c885837ad"} :step/id #uuid "297b5ab8-1639-57a8-8dcf-ef5dd67ae845" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498607}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498620 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498520 :question/id #uuid "3f77715c-0903-5f4b-a449-6816c6d9bb06"} :step/id #uuid "3ad30acf-66b9-5977-ad36-2183bb8c58c8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498619}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498623 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365659 :question/id #uuid "511649c4-cd9c-5496-8df1-bb8892a20547"} :step/id #uuid "3ea51f6a-6ea8-5162-ad6a-900689393007"