/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c007ca0-acef-5af6-b3fe-264373817071" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-1} & 2{e}^{-1} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-1}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398783} {:answer/id #uuid "59ce7ec4-615c-508f-9c8e-4b5a5d18d797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & 0 \\\\ 2t{e}^{-t} & 2{e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398784} {:answer/id #uuid "b333daa7-d3c9-5885-83dd-c52477a27945" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2t \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398785} {:answer/id #uuid "1281b7c7-c44e-5d28-ae85-689e0d3ae235" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-t} & 2t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398786} {:answer/id #uuid "de8dcb0a-6338-525e-8932-5efde71571f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-t} & 2t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398786} :answer-field/id #uuid "f91e0298-1d33-5125-a1ef-22a1c3b27757" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398782}] :question/id #uuid "6ce970ec-3f9d-57d6-9307-1253ccd1b689" :question/math-academy-id "q-334326" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x,\\,{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?" :question/worked-solution "Given a linear system ${x}^{′}=Ax,$ where $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $Φ(t)$ of the system is an $n\\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our case, we have a $2\\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}=\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}=\\begin{bmatrix}2t{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-t} & 2t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $Φ$ can be written in any order."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442556 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b36e15d-837a-5f85-9a7f-91f066b4c4fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442498} {:answer/id #uuid "434d7ea8-e63a-51b4-84d2-ca9a28788176" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & t \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442502} {:answer/id #uuid "7038d0fd-b4be-5ac4-85f1-ceeccda1e156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4} & {e}^{4} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442513} {:answer/id #uuid "a8abf479-af90-51de-b6dd-86215d035d49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442521} {:answer/id #uuid "bddbb522-2d10-5aec-9ed3-ceed476b3b3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{4t} & {e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t} & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442521} :answer-field/id #uuid "509a43d8-3e68-51c3-84c7-64c7f6910514" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442543}] :question/id #uuid "0f204e80-4d56-5e54-8e44-fafc75ad1af8" :question/math-academy-id "q-334325" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x,\\,{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?" :question/worked-solution "Given a linear system ${x}^{′}=Ax,$ where $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $Φ(t)$ of the system is an $n\\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our case, we have a $2\\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4t}=\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $Φ$ can be written in any order."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442557 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15a6f6fa-d163-59c9-8941-abbf24e3752e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442491} {:answer/id #uuid "23e9ec62-9362-5bd5-ab4e-ea2a807a4d0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & 3{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442496} {:answer/id #uuid "5416c979-f7bd-5995-af0d-eb24dc7ca789" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{2} & -{e}^{3} \\\\ {e}^{2} & 3{e}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442507} {:answer/id #uuid "83d316e3-9443-5fad-b4ad-57ed67049c56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{2t} & {e}^{2t} \\\\ -{e}^{3t} & 3{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442515} {:answer/id #uuid "ce05c0b9-0887-59c7-99c8-8f8b04b83fc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & 3{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442496} :answer-field/id #uuid "97a95112-8d67-5abc-bdfb-cee7755e29a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442549}] :question/id #uuid "5c4c6b4c-9f70-5e9c-b320-c7133b929a75" :question/math-academy-id "q-334275" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x,\\,{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?" :question/worked-solution "Given a linear system ${x}^{′}=Ax,$ where $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $Φ(t)$ of the system is an $n\\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our case, we have a $2\\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}=\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}=\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{3t} \\\\ 3{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental matrix could be written as\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & 3{e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $Φ$ can be written in any order."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442562 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2578d0cc-6b4e-585f-96d2-111f7061558f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442497} {:answer/id #uuid "37568771-74a7-5505-977e-6eec3397c6d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442499} {:answer/id #uuid "4701bacd-a91b-57c5-b9a7-cab331e6593b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442504} {:answer/id #uuid "60edb6b1-d8a3-56fd-94b1-f065189fab74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442510} {:answer/id #uuid "f1522afb-a24d-588d-823e-4a33b44528cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡1 & -{e}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡1 \\\\ {e}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡1 & {e}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442533}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442499} :answer-field/id #uuid "e91a5522-798b-57e6-88b5-94ab4c878dba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442553}] :question/id #uuid "ffe871d4-6b88-566a-ad57-5a37491576bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-334334" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x,\\,{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?" :question/worked-solution "Given a linear system ${x}^{′}=Ax,$ where $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $Φ(t)$ of the system is an $n\\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our cases, we have a $2\\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}=\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & {e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $Φ$ can be written in any order."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558553 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50015ef5-0e92-5106-aea9-ab330a93796e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558019} {:answer/id #uuid "b590d98d-384a-59d4-bdd6-f31990d7de3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{2t} & {e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558229} {:answer/id #uuid "c4fd659a-e0ec-5349-a757-959b558799ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558261} {:answer/id #uuid "d8a149ea-0805-59fc-962e-46dd8b99f2a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & t \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558294} {:answer/id #uuid "e5478b07-ac20-593b-879f-47d35b336561" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2} & {e}^{2} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558261} :answer-field/id #uuid "543370ed-7818-5d7f-878c-1a15d0e76c82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558397}] :question/id #uuid "a83f3a56-7f37-59d6-8ed6-dd0cc81608ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-334302" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x,\\,{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs and two of its linearly independent solutions ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ shown above. Which of the following is a fundamental matrix for the system?" :question/worked-solution "Given a linear system ${x}^{′}=Ax,$ where $A$ is an $n\\times n$ matrix, a *fundamental matrix* $Φ(t)$ of the system is an $n\\times n$ matrix whose columns are linearly independent solutions of the system.\n\nIn our case, we have a $2\\times 2$ system. We are also given that the two linearly independent solutions of the system are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}=[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our fundamental could be written as\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Note:** The columns of $Φ$ can be written in any order."}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Fundamental Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221421 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221193 :question/id #uuid "a52a6539-60d1-557c-a042-cbce5616e94f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21697" :question/problem "Consider the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2 \\times 2$ matrix. Let $\\Phi(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $\\Phi(t)$ satisfies the matrix differential equation $\\Phi'(t) = A \\, \\Phi(t)$ is given below.\n\n*Hint: $\\big[ \\mathbf{a}, \\: \\mathbf{b} \\big]$ denotes a matrix whose columns are vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}.$*\n\n$\\textrm{L1}{:}\\;$ Let $\\Phi(t) = \\big[ \\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\: \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\big]$\n\n$\\textrm{L2}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}_1'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}_1(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L3}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{x}_2'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}_2(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L4}{:}\\;$ $\\Phi'(t) = \\big[ \\mathbf{x}_1'(t), \\: \\mathbf{x}_2'(t) \\big]$\n\n$\\textrm{L5}{:}\\;$ $\\Phi'(t) =  \\big[ A \\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\: A \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\big]$\n\n$\\textrm{L6}{:}\\;$ $\\Phi'(t) = A \\big[ \\mathbf{x}_1(t), \\: \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\big]$\n\n$\\textrm{L7}{:}\\;$ $\\Phi'(t) = A \\Phi(t)$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L2}$ follows from the fact that ![](images/03/036695dd1a6ae9a121d25cae37b1ba7be7e77502b99335391da8d1d8d04972be.svg).\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L6}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L5}$ by ![](images/27/27f914bbca34dfde3debdcf58eb5a2205eda059ceafa10bf6964bb7c6a984d1c.svg).\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L7}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L6}$ and substitution from line ![](images/b4/b48e9d724fed6566873ba6c844fcfa10c92fcd810012da503960972f8c4105c3.svg)." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\textrm{L2}.$ Since $\\Phi(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ are linearly independent solutions of the homogeneous system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t).$ This means that each column satisfies the differential equation. In particular,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_1'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}_1(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{L2}$ follows from the fact that $\\mathbf{x}_1$ is a solution of the system.\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\textrm{L5}$ and $\\textrm{L6}.$ We now factor the matrix $A$ on the left:\n\n$$\n\\Phi'(t)=\\big[A\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\:A\\mathbf{x}_2(t)\\big]\n= A\\big[\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\:\\mathbf{x}_2(t)\\big].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{L6}$ follows from $\\textrm{L5}$ by factoring out $A$ on the left.\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\textrm{L6}$ and $\\textrm{L7}.$ From $\\textrm{L6}$ we have $\\Phi'(t)=A\\big[\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\:\\mathbf{x}_2(t)\\big].$ Using $\\Phi(t)=\\big[\\mathbf{x}_1(t),\\:\\mathbf{x}_2(t)\\big]$ from $\\textrm{L1},$ we substitute to obtain $\\Phi'(t)=A\\Phi(t).$ Thus, $\\textrm{L7}$ follows from $\\textrm{L6}$ by substitution from $L1.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0aea7503-74d6-5ba4-9411-ee53e59512d0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221990 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8335968c-9d4c-5fbb-9ffa-75dcaa53d606" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "definition of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220592} {:answer/id #uuid "b8b01ba2-8f40-5f19-b36b-a5bc0a007ec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "definition of matrix multiplication" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220593} {:answer/id #uuid "f95f3e3a-b149-5084-b607-3cd5fd0d18db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "properties of invertible matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220594} {:answer/id #uuid "6493f73f-365f-5404-a5da-5e40752fbe4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "properties of diagonalizable matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "definition of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220592} :answer-field/id #uuid "93036466-1c3c-5e59-947c-e8ff8d157797" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220591} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e243901-1d01-5ec9-9cdc-6cb103946683" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442487} {:answer/id #uuid "1dcb761f-eaed-55a9-ad0a-3da04843c7e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442494} {:answer/id #uuid "4832e2c7-75c3-5827-8346-bf8d89ce6137" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442505} {:answer/id #uuid "f947d07b-6d00-57b7-a3fe-19a08102e66a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442494} :answer-field/id #uuid "dccda4ab-5506-5030-89e0-e232b37f3a8b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220596} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "967d6780-0d69-5d91-b012-b91bfef745e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integrating the given system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220602} {:answer/id #uuid "e753d893-e42e-50d3-bb33-dac9396f5d19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the zero factor theorem" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220604} {:answer/id #uuid "ffa2aa18-1cb1-5685-99ca-77c79bd3efdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the properties of determinants" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220605} {:answer/id #uuid "ee6b0878-88fc-5098-98f5-9eeb896f07d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substituting ${Φ}^{′}=A\\,Φ$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the properties of determinants" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220605} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdb71a74-1627-56af-a5ab-668a47e38b61" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220601}] :question/id #uuid "920ced52-32ff-52a3-a836-fb31a23c82e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-333722" :question/problem "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ by {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line {{field-3}} by the properties of invertible matrices." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of the solution space for our system. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Recall that a matrix has linearly independent columns if and only if its determinant is nonzero. Since ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for any particular $t,$\n\n$$\ndet(Φ(t))\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by $\\text{the properties of determinants}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Recall that a square matrix is invertible when its determinant is nonzero. Since in $\\text{L3}$ we have that $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t,$ it follows that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t.$ Thus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ by the properties of invertible matrices."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221991 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b918900-9d47-501a-8fe9-03921f6ed92d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "properties of invertible matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305722} {:answer/id #uuid "5dbdc32e-065c-5332-9d8e-eb72d3130e00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "definition of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306051} {:answer/id #uuid "6b45a51c-d763-5bfb-9f79-8f1fadd5a426" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "definition of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306178} {:answer/id #uuid "6d2b391a-a7cf-5a3f-b395-8b5343a7d633" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "properties of diagonalizable matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306196} {:answer/id #uuid "d400de09-c2d8-5f6f-8258-d35170cbcf5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "definition of matrix multiplication" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "definition of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306051} :answer-field/id #uuid "97f4577b-63be-5c98-bcf0-c658896d4179" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307999} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c29a02a-5ddf-5ffe-8562-988f1e1a9aff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "the definition of a basis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305255} {:answer/id #uuid "316d241a-8a8a-5270-84e3-f0fc3ef40261" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "adding the vectors ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305617} {:answer/id #uuid "39f356a8-8fda-5c4b-9454-f7cb76d1ea0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integrating the given system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305702} {:answer/id #uuid "600321e5-9661-57d4-a3e1-64da86996880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "multiplying $Φ(t)$ with it's inverse" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306076} {:answer/id #uuid "e313c524-01fc-5609-ae4c-14eafceafe00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the definition of a basis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "the definition of a basis" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305255} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bd31540-fe17-5fac-bdc9-cc5602d470d7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308012} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9df8332a-0028-5851-a541-699e5fb5b445" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "definition of matrix multiplication" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306628} {:answer/id #uuid "a35b581b-96f5-5832-8aa2-3b756a1be5cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "definition of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306689} {:answer/id #uuid "b096b37f-397b-59cd-b45a-7a82f14b6d6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "fact that $Φ$ is nonsingular" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306813} {:answer/id #uuid "edcd7607-2741-5c34-acfc-0d847398cbb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "properties of invertible matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307336} {:answer/id #uuid "edd1095a-2f45-5472-aa18-be05cd67e567" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "definition of matrix multiplication" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "definition of matrix multiplication" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307337} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5be50c8-6e0b-5b31-b6e5-a6b8db5b8b63" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308245}] :question/id #uuid "7179862e-a345-5508-93d4-73732015d190" :question/math-academy-id "q-333723" :question/problem "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that every solution $x(t)$ can be written in the form $x(t)=Φ(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c=[{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}]^{T}$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of solutions\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(t)$ can be written as $x(t)={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)\\,c={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $x(t)=Φ(t)\\,c$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ by {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of the solution space for our system. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Once ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis, every solution $x(t)$ of the system can be expressed uniquely as a linear combination\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by $\\text{the definition of a basis}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Since ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are the columns of $Φ(t),$ we can write\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}(t) & {x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen by the definition of matrix multiplication,\n\n$$\nΦ(t)\\,c=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}(t) & {x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of matrix multiplication}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311648 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1666e2ac-b3fe-5abf-bf6c-480c904e35c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "properties of invertible matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308916} {:answer/id #uuid "2a0e242e-f8bc-5b61-92f4-057c108c04c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "definition of matrix multiplication" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309064} {:answer/id #uuid "39e9ae43-ec84-53c1-9ff5-1cab8a1513fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substituting ${Φ}^{′}=A\\,Φ$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309193} {:answer/id #uuid "88c4bae8-f281-5b85-b676-befbbaf6a7b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "definition of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "properties of invertible matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308916} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dd0c097-ae2f-556e-9141-4922553f97a2" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310913} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d589487-f539-5a2a-9c68-978fcbd8d916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308974} {:answer/id #uuid "4f7f3a02-4a57-5a6f-82ac-295a09b04201" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309351} {:answer/id #uuid "801400d7-5f92-5160-aa74-65afe20e1479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{A}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309754} {:answer/id #uuid "f6c6ca05-bdfb-57f3-acf6-42e584e9e189" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{Φ}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308974} :answer-field/id #uuid "60ac3f93-6295-5f30-bd13-d2e6c1a521c6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311049} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b75abdf-0c2f-5971-941e-b26da9a01df8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "properties of invertible matrices" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309435} {:answer/id #uuid "85a84435-5c8a-5585-b8e5-e8bffe321073" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substituting ${Φ}^{′}=A\\,Φ$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309787} {:answer/id #uuid "eac98a1a-0bc6-5585-badc-67bd9c21bfee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "definition of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310617} {:answer/id #uuid "fe87b04f-2863-5764-85be-e1341c2b05ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "definition of matrix multiplication" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "definition of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310617} :answer-field/id #uuid "b40a4c73-e790-56ac-96c9-d5fa994ccaf7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311314}] :question/id #uuid "3d9725c1-fcc5-57a9-8b5a-bd5efbda8207" :question/math-academy-id "q-336093" :question/problem "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ states the independence of the columns of {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line $\\text{L3}$ by the {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first interpret line $\\text{L2}.$ Line $\\text{L2}$ states that the two columns of $Φ(t)$ are linearly independent, which is required of a fundamental matrix. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ states independence of the columns of $Φ(t).$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of the solution space for our system. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Recall that a square matrix is invertible when its determinant is nonzero. Since in $\\text{L3}$ we have that $det(Φ(t))\\ne 0$ for all $t,$ it follows that $Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t.$ Thus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ by the $\\text{properties of invertible matrices}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311687 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "019f61ec-0715-5cf3-adfc-318205b19d4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "definition of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308757} {:answer/id #uuid "1372a77a-5f40-583a-81b0-59907e180c05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "definition of matrix multiplication" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308888} {:answer/id #uuid "163f0f70-1745-5d82-a520-ee8ffbaf2c9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308914} {:answer/id #uuid "4bb04a3e-77b8-5a44-b01c-eef493e8d810" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "definition of a fundamental matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308757} :answer-field/id #uuid "00d126dd-5882-5b53-ae52-aec393914e29" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310795} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ccc770b-7d17-5822-a092-2b7e33126ec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating again" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309332} {:answer/id #uuid "98050690-74a9-5e29-9fad-e82f13325cf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "factoring out $A$ on the left" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309969} {:answer/id #uuid "9a4cdbf2-f24b-58f9-8cfd-d3356bc5f95f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "adding $Φ(t)$ to both sides" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309991} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ec6680-ddc0-56bc-910c-473286d87036" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "transposing the matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "factoring out $A$ on the left" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309969} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b0ab1fe-753e-5cd2-bbcb-1248fc35e2e6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310826} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49d83357-e4a2-5f75-8ee8-f3623c8527eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "factoring out $A$ on the left" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309313} {:answer/id #uuid "67841cdb-972b-5cb7-9bb7-688577178568" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "multiplying both sides by $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309540} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec3cfef-16a5-57b7-827e-8492ef849f21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating each column" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309737} {:answer/id #uuid "fc81265a-046c-5d73-8138-ffc0ba4f84ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "transposing the matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating each column" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309737} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab49378a-bc07-5ec8-aa5b-7fade32fbd2c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311288}] :question/id #uuid "72f5b665-78cb-51a0-bf20-214a07f61466" :question/math-academy-id "q-336309" :question/problem "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ satisfies the matrix differential equation ${Φ}^{′}(t)=A\\,Φ(t)$ is given below.\n\n*Hint: $[a,\\,b]$ denotes a matrix whose columns are vectors $a$ and $b.$*\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let $Φ(t)=[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{1}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{2}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=[{x}_{1}^{′}(t),\\,{x}_{2}^{′}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=[A{x}_{1}(t),\\,A{x}_{2}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=A[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=AΦ(t)$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from the fact that $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix and line $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L5}$ by {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns are solutions of the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$ Line $\\text{L1}$ identifies ${x}_{2}(t)$ as a column of $Φ(t),$ so ${x}_{2}(t)$ must satisfy the differential equation. In particular,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from the fact that $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix and line $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Since $Φ(t)=[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)],$ the derivative of $Φ(t)$ is obtained by differentiating each column:\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=[{x}_{1}^{′}(t),\\,{x}_{2}^{′}(t)].\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by $\\text{differentiating each column}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L5}$ and $\\text{L6}.$ We now factor the matrix $A$ on the left:\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=[A{x}_{1}(t),\\,A{x}_{2}(t)]=A[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L6}$ follows from $\\text{L5}$ by $\\text{factoring out }A\\text{ on the left}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365731 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af1db821-3c70-5204-8966-bfafe5646081" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365733} {:answer/id #uuid "46a98a5b-5103-594c-8392-cbc3c0b9ab2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365734} {:answer/id #uuid "50a3b1bb-d180-5f10-92a0-b8485a60b0ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365735} {:answer/id #uuid "0df6c99a-f4dc-500f-8d10-c44449ee81e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365733} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a13d5b5-5298-5a70-9317-9289780933f2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365732} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebbe2cee-547e-5c3f-a83e-3e7039b87b6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365738} {:answer/id #uuid "0688b4ed-31ff-50f2-b750-fc4b13f37d66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365739} {:answer/id #uuid "5ae6e5f8-c049-5a31-b2e3-f3115fead233" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365740} {:answer/id #uuid "90195316-825a-515d-b3c9-3a01074b1d1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365738} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d10fc4d-7319-57d5-84c9-f456089fcfdf" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365737} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af6644a8-126d-525c-9a0e-dcbefee8db71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Φ(t)$ is invertible for all $t$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365743} {:answer/id #uuid "dda2f86b-9e7f-5832-80e9-3a25481585f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{2}$ is a solution of the system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365744} {:answer/id #uuid "014da003-c61d-5256-adef-8f54376054b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{2}$ is differentiable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365745} {:answer/id #uuid "5dad3719-55ba-50da-94ff-2a2ef7836bfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${x}_{2}$ is a solution of the system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365744} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc5120a6-040d-570c-8bef-5b5b0b3b8898" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365742}] :question/id #uuid "9e4b9ffe-ff7e-5035-97f0-e9cfaaf038a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-336308" :question/problem "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that $Φ(t)$ satisfies the matrix differential equation ${Φ}^{′}(t)=A\\,Φ(t)$ is given below.\n\n*Hint: $[a,\\,b]$ denotes a matrix whose columns are vectors $a$ and $b.$*\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let $Φ(t)=[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{1}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{2}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=[{x}_{1}^{′}(t),\\,{x}_{2}^{′}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=[A{x}_{1}(t),\\,A{x}_{2}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=A[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)]$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}(t)=AΦ(t)$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from the fact that {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from line $\\text{L4}$ and substitution from lines {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ and substitution from line {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L3}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are linearly independent solutions of the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$ This means that each column satisfies the differential equation. In particular, \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from the fact that ${x}_{2}\\text{ is a solution of the system}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L4}$ and $\\text{L5}.$ By definition, the derivative of $Φ(t)$ is obtained by differentiating each column,\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}^{′}(t) & {x}_{2}^{′}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nUsing ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{1}(t)$ from $\\text{L2}$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=A{x}_{2}(t)$ from $\\text{L3},$ we substitute each derivative into the matrix to get\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,| & | \\\\ A{x}_{1}(t) & A{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L5}$ follows from $\\text{L4}$ by substitution from $\\text{L2}\\text{ and }\\text{L3}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L6}$ and $\\text{L7}.$ From $\\text{L6}$ we have\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=A[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)].\n$$\n\nUsing the definition $Φ(t)=[{x}_{1}(t),\\,{x}_{2}(t)]$ from $\\text{L1},$ we substitute to obtain\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{′}(t)=AΦ(t).\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L7}$ follows from $\\text{L6}$ by substitution from $\\text{L1}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391264 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e315641f-7048-5d22-999e-3be3cfc5af5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186391266} {:answer/id #uuid "0233e06e-a302-51e4-b083-fe0aa